  "query_preview": "../Saturday onward/...Saturday looks to conclude our \nperiod of temperatures that have the potential to reach heat \nadvisory criteria (80F), with 850mb temperatures expected to be \naround 12C-13C, thus bringing high temperatures that are likely to\nbe in the mid to high 70s. Cloud cover on Saturday is expected to\nbe less than 25% in the morning and increase to over 50% in the \nnorthern panhandle, which could impact the temperatures that are \ncurrently forecasted. 850mb temperatures on Sunday are not \nexpected to be as warm, sitting around 10C which can still bring \ntemperatures in the 70s to the panhandle, but are not likely to \ncause advisory level heat. Temperatures in the high 60s and low \n70s are expected which marks the start of gradually cooling \ntemperatures through the week. The cause of the cooling temps is \ndue to a shift in the upper level flow to an onshore direction. \nThis shift will bring cooler, wetter air off the ocean which will \ncause a shift in the dry weather pattern we have been \nexperiencing. This shift is expected to occur through the day \nSunday, bringing our usually scheduled program back to panhandle. \nRain is expected to arrive in Yakutat Sunday evening with the \ngreatest accumulations likely to occur early Monday morning, with \nno more than 0.2 in falling in a 6 hour period. Rain will then \nmove south across the panhandle through the day Monday and is \nlikely to be showery and light in nature due to the low QPF \namounts that models are currently picking up. This pattern of \ndamper, cooler conditions is expecteted to last through most of \nnext week.",
